  <div class="wrap">
    <h1>The research behind heart coherence</h1>
    <p class="lede">Heart coherence has been studied directly with students under real test pressure, from a government-funded study of 980 10th-graders to college classrooms. Here's that research, with links to the original sources.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>What &ldquo;coherence&rdquo; actually means</h2>
    <p>Heart coherence isn't a metaphor. It's a specific, measurable pattern in heart-rate variability, defined in peer-reviewed research.</p>

    <h3>Heart Rate Variability: New Perspectives</h3>
    <p>A chaotic, irregular heart rhythm shows up as scattered energy spread across many frequencies. A coherent one compresses nearly all of that energy into a single, narrow, sine-wave-like pattern. This paper defines the coherence ratio used to tell the two apart.</p>
    <div class="study-stats">
      <div class="stat">
        <p class="stat-value">0.04&ndash;0.26 Hz</p>
        <p class="stat-label">the measurable coherence band in a heart-rate signal</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <p class="stat-value">494+</p>
        <p class="stat-label">citations by other researchers</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="meta">McCraty &amp; Shaffer, <em>Global Advances in Health and Medicine</em>, 2015. Peer-reviewed. <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4311559/">Read the study &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="section section--tint">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Research on students under test pressure</h2>
    <p class="meta">These are the studies' own published numbers, not results from Third Mind Learning students.</p>

    <h3>Emotion self-regulation and test anxiety</h3>
    <p>In an experiment measuring students' physiology directly, high schoolers who learned this technique were able to shift into a calmer state on demand, even right before a stressful test, and that calmer baseline held up over time, not just in the moment.</p>
    <div class="study-stats">
      <div class="stat">
        <p class="stat-value">136</p>
        <p class="stat-label">high-school students studied</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <p class="stat-value">New baseline</p>
        <p class="stat-label">resting coherence rose even without deliberately using the technique</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="meta">Bradley, McCraty, Atkinson, et al., <em>Applied Psychophysiology and Biofeedback</em>, 2010. Peer-reviewed. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20559707/">Read the study &rarr;</a></p>

    <h3>The TestEdge National Demonstration Study</h3>
    <p>In a U.S. Department of Education&ndash;funded study of 10th-graders at two California high schools, test anxiety was common and closely tied to lower test performance. Students who went through the program saw a real drop in test anxiety and a real gain in test performance, compared to a control group that didn't go through it.</p>
    <div class="study-stats">
      <div class="stat">
        <p class="stat-value">980</p>
        <p class="stat-label">10th-graders studied, across two California high schools</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <p class="stat-value">75%</p>
        <p class="stat-label">of anxious students reduced their test anxiety</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <p class="stat-value">10&ndash;25 pts</p>
        <p class="stat-label">test-score gain over the control group</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="meta">HeartMath Institute research report, with Claremont Graduate University. Not peer-reviewed (institute report). <a href="https://www.issuelab.org/resources/3089/3089.pdf">Read the report &rarr;</a></p>

    <h3>Self-regulation training in college freshmen</h3>
    <p>College freshmen were split three ways: this training, high-intensity exercise, or no treatment. Only the group that did this training saw less school burnout and test anxiety, along with better concentration and working memory.</p>
    <div class="study-stats">
      <div class="stat">
        <p class="stat-value">90</p>
        <p class="stat-label">college freshmen studied, across three groups</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <p class="stat-value">Best of three</p>
        <p class="stat-label">the only group of three (vs. exercise, vs. no treatment) to improve on every measure</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="meta">May, Seibert, Sanchez-Gonzalez &amp; Fincham, <em>Stress</em>, 2018. Peer-reviewed. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30345850/">Read the study &rarr;</a></p>

    <h3>Math-readiness training at a community college</h3>
    <p>Community-college students preparing for a math placement exam were taught this technique alongside their regular math instruction. Their readiness scores climbed further each year, as the training became more fully built into the course.</p>
    <div class="study-stats">
      <div class="stat">
        <p class="stat-value">+19% &rarr; +73%</p>
        <p class="stat-label">math-placement readiness gain, year one vs. year four, once fully integrated</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <p class="stat-value">Grew every year</p>
        <p class="stat-label">gains kept rising as the training became more built into the course</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="meta">Vislocky &amp; Leslie, University of Cincinnati Clermont College. Not peer-reviewed (faculty report). <a href="https://www.heartmath.org/assets/uploads/site/file/pdf/e/efficacy-and-implementation-of-heartmath-instruction.pdf">Read the report &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
